I did not plan to become a journalist.

I considered myself an artist. An advocate. Someone who talked about psychedelic therapy to hundreds of thousands of people online because it mattered, because I had seen what these medicines could do, and because almost nobody in the mainstream was saying it clearly enough.

Then I got banned. Obviously.

126,000 followers. Gone. Because the algorithm does not like the truth about mushrooms.

Then I became a mum. And for a while, my attention went where it needed to go.

But the obsession never left. It just waited.

## The Night a Golden Teacher Called Me a Journalist

During Covid, I used to sit with psilocybin mushrooms the way some people sit with prayer. Intentionally. With questions.

I would ask them things. And in that dream-state that is not quite sleep and not quite waking, they would answer.

One night, during a concert dose of Golden Teacher, they called me a journalist.

I was annoyed.

I was not a journalist. I had never been a journalist. I did not want to be a journalist. I was an artist, an advocate, a person who communicated through feeling not through reporting.

But they were insistent. Not aggressive. More like a cordyceps fungus guiding an ant exactly where it needs to go, with complete certainty and zero negotiation.

*Spread the word. Help in your way. We are medicine.*

I came back from that experience irritated and strangely clear.

## Nobody Was Doing It

When I looked at the psychedelic media landscape in late 2025, I saw a gap you could drive a truck through.

There were academic papers. There were Reddit threads. There were podcasts from people who meant well but rarely cited anything. There were American publications writing for American audiences about American policy as if the rest of the world did not exist.

There was nobody doing rigorous, independent, human psychedelic journalism from Australia.

Nobody covering the clinical trials, the policy shifts, the finance, the culture, and the history, all in one place, in plain English, with actual sources.

So I built it.

Not because it was a good business idea. Because I could not find it anywhere and I needed it to exist.

## What The Psychedelic Digest Actually Is

This is not a wellness blog. It is not a trip report forum. It is not a brand trying to sell you something adjacent to mushrooms.

The Psychedelic Digest is an independent psychedelic media platform covering therapy research, drug policy, clinical trials, psychedelic finance, and the cultural shift that is quietly rewriting mental health care globally.

It is written from Australia, for the world.

It is the blog I was told to write by a fungus during a pandemic, and I have not stopped since.

## Why It Matters Right Now

Psychedelic therapy is not a trend. It is not a moment. It is the most significant shift in psychiatry in fifty years and it is happening whether the mainstream is ready or not.

Australia became the first country to legalise psilocybin for therapeutic use. Clinical trials at Johns Hopkins, Imperial College London, and NYU are producing results that would have seemed impossible a decade ago. Governments are moving. Regulators are moving. The money is moving.

Someone needs to cover it properly. Someone who understands both the science and the sacredness. Someone who has sat with these medicines, lost a platform because of them, and come back more committed than before.

That is what this blog is.

One mushroom at a time.

